# ===========================================================================
# Google access for the OpenClaw agent. OPTIONAL add-on -- nothing in the
# base build reads this file, and the Hermes agent is unaffected by it.
#
# Copied to config/openclaw-google.env (0600). Never commit the filled-in
# copy, and never paste a value from it into a chat with an agent.
#
# This is a SEPARATE add-on from google-hermes, not a variant of it. The
# two agents reach Google by completely different mechanisms: Hermes uses
# its own bundled skill; OpenClaw uses `gog`, a CLI from the OpenClaw
# project. See PLAN.md if that surprises you.
# ===========================================================================

# --- The Google account the agent uses --------------------------------------
# Use a DEDICATED account, not your personal one. Two reasons, both
# practical: the agent's files stay out of your own Drive, and mail it
# sends comes from the agent rather than from you.
#
# What this does NOT buy you is containment. Both agents run as the same
# user inside one VM, so each can read the other's credentials from disk.
# A separate account gives you a separate identity, separate quotas and
# independent revocation -- not isolation. See PLAN.md.
GOOGLE_ACCOUNT=""

# --- Which Google services the agent may touch ------------------------------
# Comma-separated, passed to `gog auth add --services`. Consent asks for
# exactly these, so start narrow: widening later is one re-consent, and
# narrowing after the fact means revoking and starting over.
#
# Each entry maps to exactly ONE Google API you must enable in the Cloud
# Console. README.md has the table, including the service name for each --
# match on that, not on the title, because several titles are one search
# result away from a different product.
#
#   gmail     read, label, and CREATE DRAFTS. Permanent deletion is refused
#             by Google under this scope -- its rule, not ours.
#   calendar  the agent's own calendar
#   drive     required by gog for docs and sheets; not a feature in itself
#   docs      read and write documents
#   sheets    read and write spreadsheets
#   forms     read form responses
#   contacts  read AND WRITE contacts -- see the note below
#
# NOTE ON FORMS: Google publishes no read-only path that gog uses, so
# asking for forms grants `forms.body` -- which can EDIT the form, not just
# read its responses. Unavoidable, and worth knowing rather than
# discovering.
#
# NOTE ON CONTACTS: gog asks for three scopes here, not one --
# `contacts` (full read/write), `contacts.other.readonly`, and
# `directory.readonly`. So this is not "let the agent look up an address":
# it can create, edit and delete contacts, and read the directory. There is
# no read-only variant on this path. Reasonable for an agent that
# genuinely manages contacts; more than you want if it only needs to read
# them.
GOOGLE_SERVICES="gmail,calendar,drive,docs,sheets,forms,contacts"

# --- May the agent SEND email? ----------------------------------------------
# "no"  the agent can read mail and write drafts you approve and send.
# "yes" the agent can send mail on its own, to anyone.
#
# Start with "no". Drafts need no send scope, and a first live test then
# cannot accidentally email a real person. Turning this on is a re-consent,
# which takes a minute.
#
# Think about this one properly if the agent talks to people who are not
# you. A wrong message cannot be unsent, and the model will be confidently
# wrong occasionally.
GOOGLE_ALLOW_SEND="no"

# --- Form ids the agent should watch (optional) -----------------------------
# Comma-separated. Take each from the form's own URL:
#   https://docs.google.com/forms/d/THIS_PART_HERE/edit
#
# Listed explicitly on purpose. The Forms API has no "list my forms" call,
# so discovering them would mean a Drive scope that Google classifies as
# Restricted and gates behind a verification review. Naming the ids here
# keeps the grant small. Leave blank if you are not using Forms.
GOOGLE_FORM_IDS=""

# --- Keyring password -------------------------------------------------------
# LEAVE BLANK. Generated for you on first run and written back here.
#
# gog keeps its OAuth tokens in an encrypted keyring, and on a headless
# machine it will only unlock that keyring from an environment variable --
# there is no password-file or password-command option. This value is
# installed into the VM at 0600 and handed to the gog processes through a
# systemd EnvironmentFile.
#
# Be clear-eyed about what this protects: the password sits on the same
# disk, readable by the same user, as the keyring it unlocks. It satisfies
# gog's requirement; it is not a meaningful secret boundary. The Hermes
# add-on is stronger here, and PLAN.md says so.
GOOGLE_KEYRING_PASSWORD=""

# --- Written by `addon-google-openclaw.sh auth` -----------------------------
# LEAVE BLANK. Records that consent completed, so the scripts can tell a
# finished setup from an unfinished one. No token material is stored here;
# the tokens live only in the VM's keyring.
GOOGLE_AUTH_COMPLETED=""
